---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";

const metrics = [
  { value: "33.67%", label: "Bounce rate, RecordingAcademy.com" },
  { value: "2:08", label: "Average time on site there" },
  { value: "42x", label: "Awards night against an ordinary day" },
  { value: "1,857", label: "Days of pre-split baseline" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Keep the sites split, and treat the one night that carries the year as the real exposure"
  description="The Recording Academy split grammy.com and recordingacademy.com in 2022. The engagement data says the split worked, and says something more uncomfortable about the business underneath it. Case study by Linh (Min) Le."
  project="The Recording Academy"
  period="June to August 2025"
  tint="viz"
  hook="Bounce fell and sessions ran longer on the new site, so the split earned its keep. The same data put 1,389,590 visitors on awards night against 32,388 on an ordinary day."
  metrics={metrics}
  links={[
    { label: "Notebook", href: "https://github.com/minminchudu/sql-python-analytics-portfolio/blob/main/python-analysis/grammys-site-split.ipynb" },
    { label: "Repository", href: "https://github.com/minminchudu/sql-python-analytics-portfolio" },
  ]}
>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The decision this informed</h2>
        <p>
          In 2022 the Recording Academy split one web presence into two. Grammy.com would carry
          awards, music culture and ceremony content. RecordingAcademy.com would carry advocacy,
          membership and industry policy. The reasoning was that two audiences with different
          purposes should each get a site that does not make them walk past the other one&rsquo;s
          content.
        </p>
        <p>
          The question put to me was whether that had worked, and the answer had to resolve to one
          of three actions: keep the sites separate, merge them back, or do something else. A
          verdict with no action attached would have been useless, so every metric I chose had to
          be one capable of moving a decision between those three.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">The finding</h2>
        <p>
          The split worked, and the evidence is engagement quality rather than traffic volume.
          Visitors to RecordingAcademy.com bounce less often than visitors to the old combined
          site did, and they stay materially longer once they arrive.
        </p>

        <div class="cs-table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>
              Engagement across the three states of the property. The combined site is the
              pre-split baseline, 1,857 days of it, captured the same way throughout.
            </caption>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Site</th><th>Bounce rate</th><th>Average time on site</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>Combined site, before the split</th><td>41.58%</td><td>1 min 42 sec</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Grammy.com, after</th><td>40.16%</td><td>1 min 22 sec</td></tr>
              <tr class="pick"><th>RecordingAcademy.com, after</th><td>33.67%</td><td>2 min 8 sec</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Age composition points the same way. RecordingAcademy.com draws a higher share of
          visitors aged 25 to 44, while grammy.com holds more of the 55 to 64 and 65 plus
          brackets, so the two sites are reaching genuinely different rooms rather than dividing
          one audience in half.
        </p>
        <p>
          The more consequential finding was not about the split at all. Grammy.com averages
          1,389,590 visitors on the night of the ceremony against 32,388 on an ordinary day, more
          than forty times the traffic, concentrated into a single night once a year. Secondary
          spikes cluster a few weeks earlier, around nomination announcements and line-up reveals.
        </p>

        <figure class="ra-fig reveal-chart">
          <div class="ra-frame">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 700 210" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" role="img"
                 aria-label="A bar for an ordinary day at 32,388 visitors is barely visible beside a bar for awards night at 1,389,590 visitors, more than forty times taller.">
              <text class="ra-step" x="14" y="18">Average daily visitors, grammy.com</text>

              <line class="ra-axis" x1="150" y1="46" x2="150" y2="168"></line>

              <text class="ra-cat" x="142" y="76">Ordinary day</text>
              <rect class="ra-bar dim" x="150" y="60" width="15" height="22" rx="1"></rect>
              <text class="ra-val" x="175" y="76">32,388</text>

              <text class="ra-cat" x="142" y="140">Awards night</text>
              <rect class="ra-bar" x="150" y="108" width="500" height="46" rx="1"></rect>
              <text class="ra-val on" x="640" y="136">1,389,590</text>

              <text class="ra-foot" x="14" y="192">Bars to scale. One night draws more than forty times an ordinary day, and it</text>
              <text class="ra-foot" x="14" y="204">arrives once a year.</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            The engagement question was worth answering. This is the one that decides whether the
            property has a year.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          So the recommendation was to keep the two sites separate and keep tailoring each to the
          audience it now demonstrably has, while treating year-round engagement on grammy.com as
          the open problem. The site is excellent at converting one night and has no established
          reason for anyone to return in the eleven months that follow.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">What I did, and the obvious alternative I rejected</h2>
        <p>
          The data was daily web analytics for both properties: visitors, pageviews, sessions,
          bounced sessions, average session duration, and flags for awards week and awards night.
          Six years of it for grammy.com from January 2017, and RecordingAcademy.com from the
          split date of 1 February 2022.
        </p>
        <p>
          The obvious analysis is traffic. Did visitors go up after the split. I rejected it,
          because on this property traffic measures the ceremony calendar and almost nothing else.
          A single night moves the annual figure by more than forty times an ordinary day, so any
          before-and-after comparison of visitor counts mostly reports which side of the window
          caught more Grammy nights.
        </p>
        <p>
          Engagement rates survive that problem, because they are ratios within a session rather
          than counts of sessions. So the comparison ran on bounce rate, pages per session and
          average time on site, each computed the same way across all three states of the
          property, with the pre-split combined site as the baseline. Data capture on grammy.com
          was unchanged across the split, which is what makes that baseline usable at all.
        </p>
        <p>
          Of the three, I put the weight on average time on site. Pages per session did move, but
          it moved in spikes rather than shifting to a new level, and a spike is an event where a
          level shift is a change in behaviour. Reporting a spiky metric as evidence of permanent
          improvement is the easiest way to overstate a result, so it was reported and then set
          aside.
        </p>
        <p>
          pandas for loading, cleaning and the period splits, and Plotly for the visuals: a daily
          visitor time series, pages-per-session lines per domain, and a grouped bar of age
          composition across the two sites.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 class="h-sub">Assumptions and limitations</h2>
        <p>
          The pre-split baseline is one audience by construction, because the combined site served
          both. Some of RecordingAcademy.com&rsquo;s better engagement is therefore the arithmetic
          of a narrower, self-selected audience rather than of a better site. The finding that the
          split helped is sound. The size of the help is not separable from that selection effect
          with this data.
        </p>
        <p>
          Spikes that do not align with the ceremony calendar are treated as data-collection
          abnormalities, which is the brief&rsquo;s own instruction. That is a defensible rule and
          it is still a rule that discards observations, so a real anomaly falling in the same
          window would have been discarded with them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Age demographics are percentage shares rather than counts, so they describe who each
          site&rsquo;s visitors are and say nothing about how many. A site can skew younger while
          reaching fewer young people in total.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing here explains why engagement improved. These are session-level aggregates with
          no content dimension, so the analysis can establish that visitors to
          RecordingAcademy.com stay longer without identifying what holds them. Answering that
          would take page-level or category-level data the project did not include.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</CaseStudy>

